{
 "videoId": "z92soIY1rSg",
 "title": "Checkmate! That's the Tactic! | English Opening | GM Naroditsky’s Top Theory Speedrun",
 "openingName": "English Opening (reversed Sicilian)",
 "studentSide": "black",
 "voice": "danya-dna",
 "rewrittenAt": "2026-08-26",
 "source": "yt:z92soIY1rSg",
 "moves": [
  {
   "ply": 2,
   "t": 86,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/2P5/8/PP1PPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [
    "c4",
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Don't take opening shortcuts. You could slot into a Queen's-Gambit-Declined shape, but the principled move that really challenges the English is e5 — think of this whole opening as a reversed Closed Sicilian.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "e5 is the principled reply"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 96.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/2P5/2N5/PP1PPPPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 122.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/2P5/2N5/PP1PPPPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 2 3",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The bishop check on b4 leads to rich positional battles, but the simpler, more approachable move is to develop the knight to f6 — flexible, committing to nothing yet.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "the flexible Nf6"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 129,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/2P5/2N3P1/PP1PPP1P/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "g3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 130,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/2P5/2N5/PP1PPPPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 140,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/2P5/2N3P1/PP1PPP1P/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "Nf6",
    "g3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 195.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3pp3/2P5/2N3P1/PP1PPP1P/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "We play the principled d5, reaching a reversed Accelerated Dragon — an Accelerated Dragon with White a tempo up. The danger for our side is that if you just put pieces on natural squares without a thought, you drift into serious positional pressure.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "the reversed Accelerated Dragon"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 202.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3Pp3/8/2N3P1/PP1PPP1P/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "cxd5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 212,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3np3/8/2N3P1/PP1PPP1P/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "Nxd5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 215,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3np3/8/2N3P1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 1 5",
   "line": [
    "Bg2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 267.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/1n6/4p3/8/2N3P1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "Nb6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now the whole opening battle revolves around one thing: stopping the d4 break. So the knight drops back to b6, keeping its clamp on d4 — and we avoid Be6, which, down a tempo, would let White strike with d4 at once.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "prevent the d4 break",
    "Nb6 keeps the clamp"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 269.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3np3/8/2N3P1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 1 5",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 278.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/1n6/4p3/8/2N2NP1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 3 6",
   "line": [
    "Nb6",
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": "And we don't trade on c3 — in the Sicilian, and so in the reversed Sicilian too, that only boosts White's centre, helps the d4 push, and opens the b-file against our own b7-pawn.",
   "teaches": [
    "don’t trade on c3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 287,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/1nn5/4p3/8/2N2NP1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 4 7",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The knight to c6 defends b7 and, once again, guards against d4.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 294,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/1nn5/4p3/8/2N2NP1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 5 7",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 370.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/ppp1bppp/1nn5/4p3/8/2N2NP1/PP1PPPBP/R1BQ1RK1 w kq - 6 8",
   "line": [
    "Be7"
   ],
   "spoken": "The dark-squared bishop belongs on e7, and only e7 — the bishop to d6 walks into d4, and the bishop to c5 is loose, vulnerable to a3-and-b4, a knight jump to e4, or a queen x-ray from c2.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "Be7 is the safe square"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 392.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/ppp1bppp/1nn5/4p3/8/2N1PNP1/PP1P1PBP/R1BQ1RK1 b kq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": "When e3 threatens d4, reassess — pawn moves change the structure. Allowing d4 now is fine, because we trade and reach an isolated-queen-pawn structure. The shorthand for judging an IQP: who controls the square in front of the isolated pawn, and does its owner have attacking chances against the king. Both favour us here.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "the IQP structure",
    "judge the IQP by the blockade square"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 485.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/4p3/8/2N1PNP1/PP1P1PBP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 490,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/4p3/3P4/2N1PNP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "d4"
   ],
   "spoken": "We deliberately allow d4 — because in the resulting position we control the d5-square, the keys to the kingdom.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "control the blockade square"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 497,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/8/3p4/2N1PNP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "exd4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 510,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/8/3P4/2N2NP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "exd4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 513,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/8/3p4/2N1PNP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 10",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 520,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/8/3P4/2N2NP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "exd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The key: it's harder to shoot at a moving target, and we want to pile up on the square in front of the isolated pawn. But controlling that square is more important than occupying it — don't confuse the two.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "control, don’t occupy"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 553.5,
   "fen": "r2q1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn1b3/8/3P4/2N2NP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "Be6"
   ],
   "spoken": "A knight to d5 at once would allow a trade there and a knight jump to e5 with a nasty discovery on the queen. Instead the bishop to e6 quietly does the job.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "Be6 over premature Nd5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 554.5,
   "fen": "r1bq1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn5/8/3P4/2N2NP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 0 10",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 565.5,
   "fen": "r2q1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn1b3/8/3P4/2N2NP1/PP3PBP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "Be6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 614,
   "fen": "r2q1rk1/ppp1bppp/1nn1b3/8/3P4/2N2NP1/PP3PBP/R1BQR1K1 b - - 2 11",
   "line": [
    "Re1"
   ],
   "spoken": "We're a smidge better, maybe just equal — the isolated pawn isn't a big weakness since the bishop defends it, and White has the strong fianchetto bishop. But our pieces are compact and central, with many ways to intensify the pressure and bring more force to bear.",
   "kind": "review",
   "teaches": [
    "assess the IQP position honestly"
   ]
  },
